\begin{abstract}
Continuous monitoring in the Internet of Medical Things produces physiological
telemetry far faster than clinicians can read it. Generative summarization
promises relief, but it introduces a component that is slow, intermittently
unavailable, and capable of stating things the data does not contain. Placing
such a component in the ingestion path means inheriting its worst case. We
present VitalSync, an architecture that separates three concerns prior systems
couple: telemetry ingestion, deterministic risk triage, and generative
summarization. We make four contributions. First, we audit a widely downloaded
public vital-signs dataset and recover its label-generating rule exactly: a
three-clause Boolean over heart rate and body mass index reproduces all 200{,}020
published labels without a single mismatch. Classifiers reach the ceiling by
recovering that Boolean, and an ablation restricted to the remaining vital signs
performs below the majority-class baseline, so reported accuracies on this
dataset are artifacts of label construction. Second, we separate latent
physiology from sensor observation and show that a learned triage model recovers
the clean-signal NEWS2 escalation band more reliably than the NEWS2 rule applied
to the same corrupted readings, with the advantage growing monotonically in
sensor noise and reversing at zero noise, where the rule is exact by
construction. That reversal is the negative control that makes the rest
credible. Third, we show that macro-F1 selects a model reporting high-risk
observations as low risk 5.9 times more often than a cost-aware criterion, and
we report the disagreement rather than resolving it after the fact. Fourth, we
specify a summarization cascade in which a mechanical numeric-entailment check
governs fallback, terminating in a deterministic generator that cannot fail. The
implementation sustains 100 requests per second at 27--31\,ms p95 ingestion
latency, returns in under one second while the triage service is stalled for
three, and continues to ingest and summarize with that service stopped
altogether. All results are software engineering evaluation on simulated
cohorts; no clinical claim is made.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Internet of Medical Things, remote patient monitoring, early warning score,
clinical decision support, large language models, hallucination detection,
