\section{Introduction}

\IEEEPARstart{R}{emote} patient monitoring generates continuous streams of
vital-sign telemetry. The limiting factor is not acquisition. It is attention. A
ward producing thousands of readings an hour presents clinicians with far more
data than anyone can review, and the conventional response---one alarm per
parameter per threshold breach---scales with the product of patients,
parameters, and sampling rate. Studies of physiologic monitors in intensive care
have counted more than two and a half million alarms in a single month on one
unit \cite{drew2014}, with the large majority non-actionable
\cite{sendelbach2013}. The result is desensitization, and desensitization has
been linked to patient deaths.

Large language models look like an attractive remedy. They turn columns of
numbers into prose a clinician can read in seconds, and recent work reports that
adapted models can match or exceed medical experts on several clinical
summarization tasks \cite{vanveen2024}. But they also carry properties a
monitoring system should not inherit. Latency is measured in seconds rather than
milliseconds. Availability depends on a remote service that may be rate-limited
or down. And the models can produce fluent text asserting values that never
appeared in the input---a failure mode documented across summarization
generally \cite{maynez2020, kryscinski2020} and surveyed at length for natural
language generation as a whole \cite{ji2023}. A system that puts such a
component in the path of telemetry ingestion inherits all three.

VitalSync separates these concerns. Ingestion terminates at a durable write.
Risk triage is a deterministic, millisecond-scale operation that runs
independently. Generative summarization sits behind a cascade whose final tier
is a template generator with no external dependency. The architectural claim is
availability: no single language model is required for the system to function,
and no unverified number reaches a reader from a non-final tier.

There is a second thread to this paper, and it was not planned. The project was
specified around a public dataset. Before training we audited the label, which
is cheap and rarely done. What we found reframed the work: the label is a
three-clause rule over two variables, the dataset contains no abnormal reading
at all, and the high accuracies reported on it measure Boolean recovery rather
than clinical prediction. We report that as a primary contribution rather than
working around it, because the dataset is public, widely downloaded, and the
defect is invisible to the standard train-and-report workflow.

\subsection{Contributions}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item A label-provenance audit of a public vital-signs dataset that recovers
        its exact generating rule and demonstrates, by ablation, that the vital
        signs it distributes carry no information about its published label
        (Section~\ref{sec:tracka}).
  \item An experimental design that separates latent physiology from sensor
        observation, turning ``learned model versus threshold rule'' into a
        falsifiable comparison, together with the zero-noise negative control
        that validates it (Section~\ref{sec:trackb}).
  \item Evidence that aggregate classification metrics select triage models that
        are materially worse under clinically asymmetric cost, quantified on
        both a standard cohort and a reduced sensor-only deployment
        (Sections~\ref{sec:trackb} and \ref{sec:eval}).
  \item A summarization cascade in which mechanical numeric entailment governs
        fallback, with a deterministic terminal tier that guarantees a response
        (Section~\ref{sec:faith}), and an implementation whose decoupling,
        throughput, and degraded-mode behaviour are measured rather than
        asserted (Section~\ref{sec:eval}).
\end{enumerate}

