\section{Related Work and Background}
\label{sec:related}

\subsection{Early warning scores}

Aggregate early warning scores condense several vital signs into a single number
with defined escalation bands. The Modified Early Warning Score
\cite{subbe2001} established the pattern; the National Early Warning Score,
standardized by the Royal College of Physicians and revised as NEWS2
\cite{rcp2017}, is now mandated across the NHS. Its discrimination has been
measured on large observational cohorts: against the combined outcome of cardiac
arrest, unanticipated ICU admission, or death within 24 hours, NEWS achieved an
area under the receiver operating characteristic curve of 0.873, outperforming
33 competing scores \cite{smith2013}.

Scores of this kind have two properties that matter here. They are
deterministic, so a clinician can recompute one by hand from the chart. And they
are additive over parameters, so the total decomposes into contributions that
explain it. A systematic review of 95 early warning score papers found that
every included study was at high risk of bias and that many scores in daily
clinical use have real methodological weaknesses \cite{gerry2020}, which is a
caution against treating any such score as ground truth rather than as a
documented, auditable convention. We use NEWS2 in exactly that second sense.

\subsection{Machine learning for clinical deterioration}

A large literature replaces or augments these scores with learned models.
Churpek \emph{et al.} compared machine learning methods against conventional
regression for ward deterioration across multiple centres \cite{churpek2016}.
Deep models have been applied to continuous prediction of acute kidney injury at
national scale \cite{tomasev2019} and to early prediction of circulatory failure
in intensive care \cite{hyland2020}; sepsis prediction in the ICU has its own
systematic review \cite{moor2021}. Deployed at scale, automated deterioration
identification coupled to rapid-response workflows has been associated with
reduced mortality \cite{escobar2020}. The broader promise and the broader
caution are both well surveyed \cite{topol2019}.

The caution matters. External validation of a widely deployed proprietary sepsis
model found poor discrimination and calibration, with many onsets missed
\cite{wong2021}. Dataset shift between development and deployment is a
recognised clinical hazard rather than a theoretical one \cite{finlayson2021},
and the assumption that a model validated in one setting transfers to another
has been argued to be a myth \cite{futoma2020}. None of these failures is
visible from an aggregate accuracy figure on a held-out split.

\subsection{Alarm burden}

Alarm fatigue is the operational problem a triage layer is meant to address.
Observational work in intensive care documents both the raw volume and the low
actionable fraction \cite{drew2014}, and the patient-safety framing is
established \cite{sendelbach2013}. We are careful in this paper to distinguish
counting notification events, which we do, from measuring clinician workload or
fatigue, which we do not.

\subsection{Language models over clinical text}

Clinical summarization with language models is an active area, with reported
results that are genuinely strong under expert review \cite{vanveen2024}.
Faithfulness is the open problem. Large-scale human evaluation of abstractive
summarization systems found models highly prone to hallucinating content
unfaithful to the input \cite{maynez2020}, and entailment-based measures
correlate with faithfulness better than standard overlap metrics
\cite{kryscinski2020}. The general survey of hallucination in natural language
generation catalogues both the taxonomy and the mitigations \cite{ji2023}.
Retrieval augmentation reduces but does not eliminate the problem
\cite{lewis2020}.

Our contribution here is narrow and deliberately so. We do not attempt to make a
model faithful. We check its output mechanically against the numbers it was
given, and we use the result of that check to decide which tier answers.

\subsection{Machine learning systems engineering}

The engineering literature on deploying machine learning is directly relevant to
an architecture paper. Hidden technical debt in machine learning systems
\cite{sculley2015} and a survey of deployment case studies \cite{paleyes2022}
both identify the glue around the model---serving, monitoring, data
dependencies---as the dominant source of risk. Tail latency behaviour in
distributed services \cite{dean2013} motivates the bounded-executor and
timeout design we adopt: a component that is occasionally slow degrades a
coupled system far more than its average latency suggests.

\subsection{Dataset quality, leakage, and interoperability}

Leakage is a widespread failure mode in machine-learning-based science,
affecting hundreds of papers across seventeen disciplines, with a taxonomy of
eight distinct types \cite{kapoor2023}. In medical imaging specifically, a
review of COVID-19 models found none fit for clinical use, with dataset problems
prominent among the causes \cite{roberts2021}. Public critical care datasets such
as MIMIC-III \cite{johnson2016} have raised the standard for provenance by
documenting their construction. The dataset we audit in
Section~\ref{sec:tracka} does not meet that standard, and the audit is in the
same spirit as this body of work. For data exchange we follow HL7 FHIR, whose
RESTful resource model \cite{bender2013} shapes the observation contract used
throughout.

