\section{Problem Formulation and Requirements}
\label{sec:problem}

\subsection{System model}

Let a telemetry source emit observations indexed by time. Each observation is a
vector
\begin{equation}
x = (x_{\mathrm{hr}}, x_{\mathrm{spo2}}, x_{\mathrm{temp}},
     x_{\mathrm{rr}}, x_{\mathrm{sbp}}, x_{\mathrm{dbp}})
\end{equation}
of six sensed channels, optionally extended with glucose and with two
staff-recorded context flags: supplemental oxygen $o \in \{0,1\}$ and
consciousness $a \in \{0,1\}$ on the AVPU scale. Derived quantities are computed
on write:
\begin{align}
\mathrm{SI}  &= x_{\mathrm{hr}} / x_{\mathrm{sbp}}, \quad x_{\mathrm{sbp}} > 0 \\
\mathrm{PP}  &= x_{\mathrm{sbp}} - x_{\mathrm{dbp}} \\
\mathrm{MAP} &= (x_{\mathrm{sbp}} + 2 x_{\mathrm{dbp}}) / 3 .
\end{align}

A triage function $f$ maps an observation to an escalation band
$\hat{y} \in \mathcal{B} = \{\textsc{low}, \textsc{medium}, \textsc{high}\}$
with a probability vector $p \in \Delta^{2}$. A summarization function $g$ maps
an observation, a bounded trend window, and the triage output to a short text.

\subsection{Failure model}

We assume the following may occur independently and at any time, and the system
must remain useful under each.

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{F1, triage unavailable.} The service implementing $f$ is
        unreachable, returns an error, or exceeds its timeout.
  \item \textbf{F2, generator unavailable.} A tier implementing $g$ is
        unreachable, rate-limited, or exceeds its timeout.
  \item \textbf{F3, generator unfaithful.} A tier returns well-formed text that
        asserts a value absent from its inputs, or names a condition.
  \item \textbf{F4, overload.} Offered telemetry exceeds the rate at which
        analysis can complete.
  \item \textbf{F5, incomplete observation.} One or more channels are missing,
        or the staff-recorded flags are unavailable because the feed is
        sensor-only.
\end{itemize}

F3 is the failure that motivates most of this paper, because unlike the others
it is silent. A tier that is down announces itself. A tier that invents a
plausible lactate value does not.

\subsection{Requirements}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item[R1] \textbf{Ingestion independence.} Acknowledgement latency must not
        depend on the latency of $f$ or $g$.
  \item[R2] \textbf{Bounded resource use.} Under F4 the system must apply
        backpressure rather than accumulate unbounded work.
  \item[R3] \textbf{Honest degradation.} Under F1 the band must be reported as
        unavailable, never inferred.
  \item[R4] \textbf{Total summarization.} Under F2 and F3 a summary must still
        be produced.
  \item[R5] \textbf{Grounded output.} No text from a non-final tier may assert a
        value absent from its inputs.
  \item[R6] \textbf{Auditability.} Every assessment must record which tier
        answered, whether triage was reachable, and whether verification passed.
\end{enumerate}

Section~\ref{sec:eval} evaluates each of these against the implementation.

