\section{Track A: Auditing the Dataset}
\label{sec:tracka}

\subsection{Motivation}

The project was specified around a public Kaggle dataset of human vital
signs~\cite{dataset} (200{,}020 rows, 17 columns) carrying a
\texttt{Risk Category} label, to be classified with random forest and gradient
boosting. Before training we audited the label. This is cheap, and given the
documented prevalence of leakage \cite{kapoor2023} and dataset-driven failure
\cite{roberts2021}, it should be routine.

\subsection{Method}

We computed per-class descriptive statistics for every numeric column, ranked
features by mutual information with the label, fitted a depth-3 decision tree and
exported its rule, tested a candidate Boolean against all rows, reproduced the
specified random forest and gradient boosting experiment, and ran an ablation
restricted to the vital signs that showed no class separation.

\subsection{Result: the label is a rule}

The published label is reproduced exactly, with zero mismatches across all
200{,}020 rows, by
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{High} \iff (\mathrm{HR} > 90) \lor (\mathrm{BMI} > 30) \lor
(\mathrm{BMI} < 18.5).
\label{eq:rule}
\end{equation}

Three consequences follow, in increasing order of damage.

The dataset is binary. There is no \textsc{normal} class, so the three-class
target originally specified cannot be trained from it at all.

Every vital sign lies inside its normal reference range: heart rate 60--99,
oxygen saturation 95.0--100.0, systolic pressure 110--139, temperature
36.0--37.5, respiratory rate 12--19. The cohort contains no physiologically
abnormal observation. It is a dataset of healthy people with a label attached.

Class-conditional means for oxygen saturation, both blood pressures,
temperature, respiratory rate, and mean arterial pressure agree to three decimal
places. Those variables carry no label information whatsoever.

Table~\ref{tab:audit} and Fig.~\ref{fig:audit} report the classification results
alongside the rule and the ablation. Both ensembles reach the ceiling. The
ablation, trained only on oxygen saturation, blood pressure, temperature, and
respiratory rate, does not beat the majority-class baseline.

\input{tables/table_audit}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_audit.pdf}
\caption{Track A. Both ensembles reach the ceiling by recovering the label rule.
The ablation trained only on oxygen saturation, blood pressure, temperature and
respiratory rate falls \emph{below} the majority-class baseline: those channels
carry no information about the published label.}
\label{fig:audit}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Implication}

A classification score on this dataset measures recovery of
Equation~\ref{eq:rule}. It is not evidence of clinical predictive capability. A
reviewer fitting a shallow tree finds this in seconds, which makes publishing
such a score a reputational risk as much as a scientific one. We therefore do
not use this dataset to train the deployed model, and we recommend that any
classification result on a public physiological dataset be accompanied by
evidence that the label is not a deterministic function of the features.

