\section{Track B: Triage Under Sensor Noise}
\label{sec:trackb}

\subsection{The circularity problem}

Substituting NEWS2 for the dataset's label does not by itself solve anything.
NEWS2 is also a deterministic function of vital signs, so a model trained to
reproduce NEWS2 from clean vitals is learning a rule that is already known, and
any reported accuracy is a statement about function approximation rather than
about clinical utility. Swapping one rule for another changes which tautology is
being measured, not whether one is.

\subsection{Design: latent truth versus observation}

We break the circularity by distinguishing what is true from what is measured:
\[
x^{*} \xrightarrow{\ \mathrm{NEWS2}\ } y^{*}, \qquad
x^{*} \xrightarrow{\ \text{sensor noise}\ } \hat{x},
\]
where $x^{*}$ is latent physiology, $y^{*}$ the ground-truth escalation band,
and $\hat{x}$ the corrupted telemetry. Ground truth is scored on $x^{*}$. Every
method---including the rule baseline---observes only $\hat{x}$. The rule
baseline computes NEWS2 on $\hat{x}$, which is precisely what a conventional
threshold monitor does in practice. The learned model is trained on
$(\hat{x}, y^{*})$ and can therefore internalize the noise distribution, soften
band boundaries, and exploit cross-parameter redundancy that a per-parameter
rule cannot.

This yields a falsifiable comparison with a built-in negative control: at zero
noise $\hat{x} = x^{*}$, the rule is exact by construction, and the learned
model \emph{must not} beat it. Fig.~\ref{fig:design} shows the arrangement.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\input{figures/tikz_design}
\caption{Separating latent physiology from sensor observation. Ground truth is
scored on the latent signal; every method, including the rule baseline, observes
only the corrupted telemetry; the dashed edges indicate that both are scored
against the same latent-signal truth. This is what makes ``learned model versus
threshold rule'' a real comparison rather than a restatement of the labelling
function.}
\label{fig:design}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Cohort and corruption model}

Latent severity $z \in [0,1]$ is drawn from a three-component mixture: 60\%
stable, 25\% mild derangement, 15\% acute deterioration, with Beta shapes
keeping each component inside its own region. Four deterioration archetypes
(respiratory, circulatory, febrile, mixed) weight the channels differently, so
that deterioration appears across several parameters at once rather than in one
channel independently. This matters: a generator with independent marginals
would make the aggregate score trivially redundant and would flatter any method
that exploits aggregation.

Corruption is applied per channel: Gaussian noise on all channels, motion
artifact on heart rate at 1.5\% of readings, perfusion dropout on oxygen
saturation at 2\%, display quantization, and 3\% whole-channel dropout imputed at
inference. Quantization is treated as part of the corruption and is disabled at
zero noise, so that the zero-noise condition is an exact identity and can serve
as a test of the generator and scorer themselves. Appendix~\ref{app:noise} gives
the parameters.

\subsection{Metrics}

All estimators are implemented with scikit-learn~\cite{pedregosa2011}. The
random forest \cite{breiman2001} and gradient boosting \cite{friedman2001}
configurations follow the original project specification; related gradient
boosting implementations are surveyed in \cite{chen2016}.

Macro-F1 is the pre-registered selection criterion. It is insufficient alone: it
scores a missed high-risk observation identically to a spurious one. We
therefore also report high-risk recall, \emph{missed-HIGH} (the fraction of
high-risk observations reported as low risk), and a mean cost under an
asymmetric matrix penalizing under-triage far more heavily than over-triage,
following the cost-sensitive learning framework of Elkan \cite{elkan2001}. Those
weights are a stated assumption, not derived from outcome data;
Fig.~\ref{fig:cost} shows the matrix and Appendix~\ref{app:cost} states it
explicitly.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_cost_matrix.pdf}
\caption{The asymmetric cost matrix. Rows are the true band, columns the
predicted band. The lower-left cell, a high-risk observation reported as low
risk, carries 25 times the cost of a one-band over-triage. These weights encode
a clinical intuition and are not derived from outcome data.}
\label{fig:cost}
\end{figure}

Calibration is assessed by expected calibration error over ten equal-width bins
and by the multiclass Brier score, following standard practice
\cite{guo2017, niculescu2005}. A confidence value displayed to a clinician that
does not mean what it says is worse than displaying none.

\subsection{Results}

Table~\ref{tab:trackb} reports all five methods on the same corrupted
observations. Table~\ref{tab:noise} and Fig.~\ref{fig:noise} sweep the noise
scale, averaging three independent cohort seeds per cell and reporting a 95\%
confidence interval on the model-minus-rule difference.

