\subsection{Selection criteria disagree}

Macro-F1 and the cost criterion select different models. The F1-optimal model
attains higher aggregate scores while reporting high-risk observations as low
risk 5.9 times more often than the cost-optimal alternative, for a macro-F1
difference of less than $0.006$. Fig.~\ref{fig:missedhigh} shows that the gap
persists across the whole noise sweep rather than being an artifact of one
operating point.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_missed_high.pdf}
\caption{Missed-HIGH rate across the noise sweep: the fraction of genuinely
high-risk observations reported as low risk. This is the error the aggregate
metrics hide, and it is the one a triage system exists to prevent.}
\label{fig:missedhigh}
\end{figure}

We report the disagreement and retain the pre-registered criterion. Changing it
after observing results would be precisely the post-hoc selection that makes a
reported number untrustworthy, and the project's own audit of someone else's
dataset would sit badly beside it.

\subsection{Calibration}

The system displays a confidence value, so whether that value means anything is
part of the result rather than an implementation detail.
Table~\ref{tab:calibration} and Fig.~\ref{fig:calibration} give the reliability
table and diagram. We expected over-confidence, since tree ensembles are known
to produce voting fractions that read like probabilities without being
calibrated \cite{niculescu2005}. We did not find much: expected calibration
error is 0.0053 and mean confidence exceeds observed accuracy by 0.0014. The
point is not that the suspicion was wrong. It is that the system previously
displayed a number nobody had checked.

