\section{System Architecture}
\label{sec:arch}

\subsection{Overview}

Four components: a Python triage service exposing a stateless inference API; a
Java backend owning authentication, validation, persistence, orchestration and
the summarization cascade; PostgreSQL for durable telemetry and assessments; and
a browser dashboard. Fig.~\ref{fig:arch} shows the arrangement and, more
importantly, the boundary between what must be fast and what is permitted to be
slow.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\input{figures/tikz_architecture}
\caption{Architecture and the synchronous/asynchronous boundary. Ingestion
performs one durable write and returns \texttt{202 Accepted}; triage and
summarization proceed on a bounded executor behind it. Ingestion throughput is
therefore bounded by the database, never by whichever language model is
currently answering.}
\label{fig:arch}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{The decoupling rule}

Telemetry ingestion performs validation and one durable write, then returns
\texttt{202 Accepted}. The status code is deliberate. Returning \texttt{200}
would imply the analysis was complete, which under load it is not.

Analysis is handed to a bounded executor with a caller-runs rejection policy.
The bound matters more than it first appears. An unbounded queue would let a
telemetry burst accumulate an ever-growing backlog of pending model calls and
eventually exhaust heap, converting a load spike into an outage. With a bounded
queue and caller-runs, sustained overload instead pushes back on the ingestion
thread: ingestion slows, nothing is dropped, and nothing grows without limit.
This is requirement R2, and it follows the tail-latency reasoning of
\cite{dean2013}---an occasionally slow dependency damages a coupled system far
more than its mean latency suggests.

Fig.~\ref{fig:sequence} shows the timing for a single reading, with the point of
acknowledgement marked.

