\section{Faithfulness-Verified Summarization}
\label{sec:faith}

\subsection{Why prompting is not enough}

The prompt instructs the model not to invent findings. Prompts are not
enforcement. The summarization literature is unambiguous that instruction
following does not prevent hallucination \cite{maynez2020, ji2023}, and in a
clinical context the specific failure that matters is a fabricated number,
because a number looks checkable and therefore invites trust.

\subsection{Definition}

Let $R$ be a summarization request carrying the latest reading, the derived
features, the NEWS2 result, and trend deltas over a bounded window. Define the
\emph{supported set} $\mathcal{A}(R)$ as the union of all values the request
legitimately contains, together with their rounded forms at integer, one-decimal
and two-decimal precision, and the reference-range constants the deterministic
tier itself quotes.

A candidate summary $T$ is \emph{faithful} with respect to $R$ when
\begin{equation}
\forall v \in \mathrm{num}(T) \;\; \exists\, \alpha \in \mathcal{A}(R)
: \; |v - \alpha| \le \tau
\label{eq:faith}
\end{equation}
and $T$ contains no term from a list of condition names, where $\mathrm{num}(T)$
extracts numeric literals from $T$ after removing measurement names that end in
a digit, and $\tau$ is an absolute tolerance.

\subsection{Choosing the tolerance}

The tolerance is the whole design. Set it loose and the check is decorative; set
it tight and legitimate rounding is rejected.

Our first implementation used $\tau = 1.0$, which seemed conservative. It was
not. A test summary inventing ``lactate 4.7 mmol/L'' passed, because the
patient's NEWS2 total was 5 and $|4.7 - 5| = 0.3 < \tau$. A fabricated clinical
value had matched an unrelated score. The lesson generalises: a tolerance tuned
against large values is a hole at small ones. One unit on a heart rate of 118 is
0.8\%; on a score of 5 it is 20\%.

The fix was to enumerate the legitimate rounded forms explicitly in
$\mathcal{A}(R)$ and then reduce $\tau$ to $0.05$, which now absorbs only
floating-point representation error. That case is retained as a regression test.

\subsection{Cascade}

Algorithm~\ref{alg:cascade} gives the procedure, and Fig.~\ref{fig:cascade}
the flow. Two properties follow directly. Failing verification is treated as a
tier failure rather than as a result, so a fabricating tier is skipped rather
than surfaced (R5). And the terminal tier has no external dependency and is
verified in test to satisfy Equation~\ref{eq:faith} by construction, so the loop
always terminates with a summary (R4).

\begin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{Verification-gated summarization cascade}
\label{alg:cascade}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\STATE \textbf{Input:} request $R$; ordered tiers $g_1, \dots, g_n$ with $g_n$
       deterministic
\STATE \textbf{Output:} summary $T$, tier identifier, verification result
\FOR{$i = 1$ \TO $n$}
  \IF{$g_i$ is not configured or not reachable}
    \STATE \textbf{continue}
  \ENDIF
  \STATE $T \leftarrow g_i(R)$ \hfill \COMMENT{never throws; may return $\emptyset$}
  \IF{$T = \emptyset$ \OR $T$ has fewer than three bullets}
    \STATE \textbf{continue} \hfill \COMMENT{F2: tier failure}
  \ENDIF
  \STATE $\phi \leftarrow \textsc{Faithful}(T, R)$ by Eq.~\ref{eq:faith}
  \IF{$\neg \phi$ \AND $i < n$}
    \STATE \textbf{continue} \hfill \COMMENT{F3: treated as tier failure}
  \ENDIF
  \STATE \textbf{return} $(T, i, \phi)$
\ENDFOR
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\input{figures/tikz_cascade}
\caption{Summarization cascade. A tier that fails, times out, or returns output
failing the faithfulness check is skipped. The deterministic tier has no
external dependency and therefore always produces a summary, which is the
property the availability claim rests on.}
\label{fig:cascade}
\end{figure}

\subsection{What this does not do}

The check is necessary, not sufficient, and we want to be explicit about the
gap. It catches invented values and named conditions. It cannot detect a
summary that is fluent, numerically correct, and clinically wrong---a correct
recitation of the numbers paired with a misleading emphasis would pass. Nor is
it a substitute for clinician review. Entailment-based approaches
\cite{kryscinski2020} would extend the check beyond numerals, and we regard that
as the natural next step rather than as a solved problem.

