\section{Implementation}
\label{sec:impl}

The triage service is Python with scikit-learn \cite{pedregosa2011}, serving one
persisted pipeline that contains the imputer, the scaler and the estimator in a
single artifact. Shipping these separately is a well-known route to silent
train/serve skew \cite{sculley2015}; one artifact makes the skew impossible. A
model card recording the artifact hash, the selection metric, the headline
metrics and the intended use is stored alongside it.

The backend is Java with Spring Boot. Persistence uses explicit SQL through
\texttt{JdbcClient} with Flyway migrations rather than an object-relational
mapper, because the headline claim concerns latency and hidden lazy-loading
behaviour would undermine the measurements. The executor is configured with core
4, maximum 8, queue 500, and a caller-runs rejection policy. Outbound calls to
the triage service carry a 2\,s connect and 5\,s read timeout; without explicit
timeouts a stalled dependency would hold an analysis thread indefinitely and
eventually exhaust the pool.

Both language model tiers are optional beans. With neither configured the
application starts normally and the deterministic tier answers every request.
This is not an incidental property: it is what allows the system to be deployed,
tested and reproduced with no API keys and no local model weights.

The dashboard is served by the backend as static assets with no build step. It
surfaces the ward sorted by acuity, the per-parameter NEWS2 breakdown beside the
model band, and---deliberately---which tier produced each summary and whether it
passed verification.

