\section{Evaluation}
\label{sec:eval}

\subsection{Triage latency}

Triage is the only model-dependent step in the synchronous-facing path, so its
cost bounds how much of the ingestion budget analysis can consume.
Table~\ref{tab:latency} reports the in-process model call over 1000
single-observation predictions. The distribution is tight and the tail is short,
which is what permits analysis to be scheduled per reading rather than batched.

\input{tables/table_latency}

\subsection{R1: decoupling under a stalled dependency}

With the triage service artificially stalled for 3\,s, ingestion returns in
under 1\,s. A synchronous implementation cannot pass this test, which is why it
is the load-bearing test of the architectural claim.

This test also taught us something about measurement. The first version failed,
and the tempting conclusion was that the asynchronous dispatch was broken. It
was not. The test was performing an authentication round-trip inside the timed
region, and on first call also paying Spring Security filter initialisation and
JIT warm-up. Hoisting both out of the measurement and tightening the bound from
1500\,ms to 1000\,ms produced a test that passes comfortably and measures what it
claims. Changing working architecture to satisfy a broken measurement would have
been the worse outcome by some distance.

\subsection{R2: throughput and saturation}

Offered load was generated open-loop. This matters: a closed-loop generator that
waits for each reply before sending the next silently throttles itself to
whatever the server can manage, and therefore cannot detect saturation at all.
With a fixed schedule, a server that falls behind manifests as rising latency.

At 10, 50 and 100 requests per second the system tracks the offered rate with
p95 ingestion latency between 27 and 31\,ms. At an offered 250 it saturates,
achieving approximately 159 requests per second with p95 latency rising by two
orders of magnitude. The asynchronous analysis backlog drained completely at
every rate, which is the behaviour requirement R2 describes: the system slows
rather than failing or accumulating unbounded work.
Fig.~\ref{fig:throughput} shows both panels; achieved rate and latency are
plotted separately rather than on twin axes, since they share no scale.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_throughput.pdf}
\caption{Ingestion under open-loop offered load. Achieved rate tracks offered
load to 100 requests per second and then departs from it; p95 latency rises by
two orders of magnitude at the saturation point. Latency uses a logarithmic
axis.}
\label{fig:throughput}
\end{figure}

\subsection{R3, R4, R6: resilience}

Three conditions were exercised by stopping and restarting containers rather
than by mocking. With all services running, every reading produced an
assessment. With the triage service stopped, ingestion and summarization both
remained at 100\%, assessments were recorded with the model marked unavailable
and the band reported as unavailable rather than guessed, and summaries were
produced by the deterministic tier. On restart the system resumed using the
model without a backend restart.

The recovery condition initially reported a false failure, which is worth
recording. The readiness check polled the triage container's own health endpoint
from the host, which answers the question ``is the process listening'' rather
than the question that matters, ``can the backend obtain a prediction''.
Replacing it with an end-to-end probe---submit a reading, wait for an assessment
with the model flag set---produced the correct result. Readiness is properly
defined from the consumer's point of view.

\subsection{R5: faithfulness enforcement}

Under test, a candidate summary inventing a lactate value is rejected and the
cascade falls through; the deterministic tier is verified to pass the same check
on normal, severely deranged, bradycardic and hypertensive inputs. In live
operation every summary recorded so far has been produced by the deterministic
tier and has passed, because no language model has been enabled. This is a real
limitation and is discussed in Section~\ref{sec:limits}.

