\section{Discussion}

\subsection{On auditing before modeling}

The Track A finding was not subtle and required no sophisticated tooling. A
depth-3 tree exposes it in seconds. It survives in the literature because the
standard workflow never asks the question. Given that leakage has been
documented across hundreds of papers in seventeen disciplines \cite{kapoor2023},
we suggest that a label-provenance check should be as routine as a train/test
split, and that it is cheap enough that there is no good reason to skip it.

\subsection{On negative controls}

The $\sigma = 0$ result, where the learned model is significantly worse than the
rule, is the most informative number in Table~\ref{tab:noise}. A design in which
the model wins everywhere would be indistinguishable from a design that leaks.
This connects to the generalizability critique \cite{futoma2020}: a result that
cannot fail under any condition is not evidence about the conditions it claims
to describe.

\subsection{On aggregate metrics in clinical triage}

Macro-F1 selected the worse model twice in this work, under different
circumstances: once between estimator families, once between input
configurations. In both cases the cost-relevant difference was invisible in the
aggregate score. Where error costs are asymmetric---and in triage they always
are---directional error rates belong alongside aggregate metrics, not in an
appendix. The cost-sensitive framing is old \cite{elkan2001}; the observation
here is that it is still routinely omitted from reported results.

\subsection{On architecture as a safety property}

It is tempting to treat the decoupling as an engineering convenience. It is not.
Under F1 a coupled system has two options, both bad: block ingestion, or
fabricate a band. The architecture removes the dilemma by making the degraded
state representable and durable. Much of what this paper calls resilience is
really just refusing to let an unavailable component be silently replaced by a
guess.

%% ======================================================================
\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limits}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item The Track B cohort is simulated. Results are software evaluation, not
        clinical validation, and the corruption model is a stated assumption
        rather than a measured device characterisation.
  \item No language model has been run live. Section~\ref{sec:llm} is
        unpopulated and the faithfulness guard is unvalidated against real
        generative output.
  \item Faithfulness checking cannot detect fluent, numerically correct,
        clinically incorrect interpretation.
  \item The asymmetric cost weights encode intuition, not outcome data.
  \item NEWS2 is validated for acutely ill adults in hospital
        \cite{smith2013, rcp2017}; applying it to simulated continuous wearable
        telemetry is outside its validation scope, and the methodological
        critique of early warning scores \cite{gerry2020} applies.
  \item A sensor-only deployment loses two of seven NEWS2 parameters, with the
        measured consequences in Table~\ref{tab:wearable}.
  \item Security is prototype grade: a single demo credential, no refresh or
        revocation, no external identity provider, no TLS in the bundled stack.
  \item Throughput figures characterize one workstation and are not service
        level objectives.
  \item The evaluation uses a single simulated cohort generator. Dataset shift
        between a generator and reality is exactly the hazard described in
        \cite{finlayson2021}, and nothing here measures it.
\end{enumerate}

%% ======================================================================
\section{Ethics and Safety}

The system is a research prototype and not a medical device. It produces
software triage categories, not diagnoses, and every API response and every
screen carries that statement. No real patient data was used at any stage; all
cohorts are simulated and the audited public dataset contains no identifiable
information.

Enabling the cloud summarization tier transmits telemetry to a third party,
which is acceptable for synthetic data and would require a data processing
agreement otherwise. Deployment on real data would require the hardening
enumerated under Limitations, and a determination of regulatory status under
applicable Clinical Decision Support guidance \cite{fda2026}. Risk management
for the generative component would follow the NIST generative AI profile
\cite{nist2024}.

%% ======================================================================
\section{Reproducibility}

All results are regenerated from a clean checkout by a single script. Each
experiment writes a JSON artifact recording its seeds, configuration and
environment; every table and figure in this paper is generated from those
artifacts, and no number is entered by hand. The audited dataset is not
redistributed; a resolver script locates it and records its SHA-256 so a result
can be tied to a specific file. Track B requires no external data.

%% ======================================================================
\section{Conclusion}

VitalSync demonstrates that continuous telemetry ingestion and low-latency risk
triage can be isolated from generative summarization, remaining fully available
when every language model tier is unreachable, and that generated clinical text
can be held to a mechanical grounding check that governs fallback rather than
merely annotating output.

Along the way we found that the public dataset the work was premised on carries
a label that is a deterministic rule over two variables, and we report that as a
caution about label provenance. Separating latent physiology from sensor
observation gives a falsifiable comparison in which a learned model outperforms
a threshold rule only under observation noise, and demonstrably fails to when
there is none. Finally, we show that aggregate classification metrics can select
triage models that are materially worse under asymmetric clinical cost.

Evaluating the generative tiers themselves remains open, and is marked as such.
So is the obvious next step for the verification gate: extending it from numeric
entailment to textual entailment, so that it catches claims the current check
cannot see.

