\section{Sensor Corruption Parameters}
\label{app:noise}

Per-channel corruption at noise scale $\sigma = 1$: Gaussian standard deviations
of 2.5\,bpm (heart rate), 1.5\% (oxygen saturation), 1.5\,min$^{-1}$
(respiratory rate), 0.15\,$^{\circ}$C (temperature), 5\,mmHg (systolic) and
4\,mmHg (diastolic). Motion artifact is applied to 1.5\% of heart-rate readings,
adding 15--40\,bpm. Perfusion dropout is applied to 2\% of oxygen-saturation
readings, subtracting 3--8 percentage points. Whole-channel dropout occurs at
3\% per channel and is median-imputed at inference. Display quantization is
applied to integers for heart rate, respiratory rate and both pressures, and to
one decimal for oxygen saturation and temperature.

Quantization is part of the corruption model and is disabled at $\sigma = 0$.
Without that, rounding alone pushes values across NEWS2 thresholds and the
zero-noise identity fails at approximately 0.984 rather than exactly 1, which is
how the issue was originally found.

\section{Cost Matrix}
\label{app:cost}

The asymmetric cost matrix $C$, indexed $C[\text{true}][\text{predicted}]$ over
$(\textsc{low}, \textsc{medium}, \textsc{high})$:
\begin{equation}
C =
\begin{bmatrix}
0 & 1 & 2 \\
5 & 0 & 1 \\
25 & 5 & 0
\end{bmatrix}
\end{equation}

Under-triage is penalised more heavily than over-triage, and missing a
high-risk observation entirely is penalised an order of magnitude beyond a
one-band slip. These weights are an assumption. They encode the view that
escalating unnecessarily costs a review while failing to escalate can cost a
patient, but they are not derived from outcome data and a reader who disagrees
should substitute their own and rerun.



